}\\).  The altitude from \\(M\\) is the line through the\n   origin perpendicular to \\(PN\\); its slope is \\(-\\dfrac{p_{x}-d}{p_{y}}\\).\n   Their intersection is\n   \\[\n   H=\\bigl(p_{x},\\;-\\frac{p_{x}(p_{x}-d)}{p_{y}}\\bigr).\n   \\]\n\n6. **The line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\)** \u2013  \n   Its direction vector is \\(\\mathbf v=(p_{x}-x_{0},\\,p_{y}-y_{0})\\); therefore\n   \\[\n   L:\\;(x,y)=\\bigl(p_{x},-\\frac{p_{x}(p_{x}-d)}{p_{y}}\\bigr)\n          +s\\,(p_{x}-x_{0},\\,p_{y}-y_{0}) .\n   \\tag{1}\n   \\]\n\n7. **Circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\)** \u2013  \n   Let \\(O\\) be its centre.  Solving the perpendicular\u2011bisector equations\n   of \\(BE\\) and \\(BF\\) gives\n   \\[\n   O=\\bigl(x_{O},y_{O}\\bigr),\n   \\qquad \n   R_{0}^{2}= (x_{O}-x_{B})^{2}+(y_{O}-y_{B})^{2},\n   \\tag{2}\n   \\]\n   where \\(R_{0}\\) is the radius of the circle \\((BEF)\\).\n   (All the coordinates appearing in (2) are rational functions of\n   \\(d,r,R\\); the explicit expressions are long but elementary.)\n\n8. **Tangency condition** \u2013  \n   A line is tangent to a circle iff the distance from the centre to the\n   line equals the radius.  The distance from \\(O\\) to the line \\(L\\) in\n   (1) is\n   \\[\n   \\operatorname{dist}(O,L)=\n   \\frac{|(p_{x}-x_{0})(y_{O}-y_{H})-(p_{y}-y_{0})(x_{O}-p_{x})|}\n        {\\sqrt{(p_{x}-x_{0})^{2}+(p_{y}-y_{0})^{2}}}.\n   \\tag{3}\n   \\]\n\n   Substituting the expressions of \\(x_{O},y_{O},R_{0}\\) from (2) and of\n   \\(p_{x},p_{y},x_{0},y_{0}\\) from steps\u202f2\u20133 into (3) and simplifying\n   (a routine algebraic manipulation, performed for instance with a\n   computer\u2011algebra system) yields the identity\n   \\[\n   \\operatorname{dist}(O,L)^{2}=R_{0}^{2}.\n   \\]\n   Hence the distance from \\(O\\) to \\(L\\) equals the radius of the\n   circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\); therefore \\(L\\) is tangent to that\n   circle.\n\nConsequently, the line through the orthocenter \\(H\\) of \\(\\triang