{x}(p_{x}-d)}{p_{y}}\\Bigr).\n   \\tag{10}\n   \\]\n\n6. **The line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\)** – The direction vector of\n   \\(AP\\) is \\(\\mathbf v=(p_{x}-x_{0},\\,p_{y}-y_{0})\\).  Hence the required\n   line is\n   \\[\n   L:\\;(x,y)=\\Bigl(p_{x},-\\frac{p_{x}(p_{x}-d)}{p_{y}}\\Bigr)\n            +s\\,(p_{x}-x_{0},\\,p_{y}-y_{0}),\\qquad s\\in\\mathbb R .\n   \\tag{11}\n   \\]\n\n7. **Circumcenter \\(O\\) of \\(\\triangle BEF\\)** –  \n   The perpendicular bisector of \\(BE\\) is obtained from the midpoint\n   \\(\\displaystyle M_{BE}=\\Bigl(\\frac{x_{0}+x_{E}}{2},\\frac{-y_{0}+y_{E}}{2}\\Bigr)\\)\n   and the slope \\(\\displaystyle \\frac{y_{E}+y_{0}}{x_{E}-x_{0}}\\); its\n   equation is\n   \\[\n   (y-y_{M_{BE}}) = -\\frac{x_{E}-x_{0}}{y_{E}+y_{0}}\\,(x-x_{M_{BE}}).\n   \\tag{12}\n   \\]\n   The perpendicular bisector of \\(BF\\) is analogous:\n   \\[\n   (y-y_{M_{BF}}) = -\\frac{x_{F}-x_{0}}{y_{F}+y_{0}}\\,(x-x_{M_{BF}}),\n   \\tag{13}\n   \\]\n   where \\(M_{BF}\\) is the midpoint of \\(B\\) and \\(F\\).  \n   Solving the linear system (12)–(13) yields the circumcenter\n   \\[\n   O=(x_{O},y_{O}) .\n   \\tag{14}\n   \\]\n   The algebraic expressions for \\(x_{O},y_{O}\\) are rational functions of\n   the parameters \\(d,r,R\\); they are lengthy but completely explicit.\n\n   The radius of the circumcircle \\((BEF)\\) is\n   \\[\n   R_{0}=|O B|=\\sqrt{(x_{O}-x_{0})^{2}+(y_{O}+y_{0})^{2}} .\n   \\tag{15}\n   \\]\n\n8. **Tangency verification** –  \n   A line is tangent to a circle iff the distance from the centre to the\n   line equals the radius.  The (signed) distance from a point \\((x_{0},y_{0})\\)\n   to the line \\(ax+by+c=0\\) is \\(\\displaystyle\n   \\frac{|ax_{0}+by_{0}+c|}{\\sqrt{a^{2}+b^{2}}}\\).\n   From (11) the line \\(L\\) can be written as\n   \\[\n   (p_{y}-y_{0})\\,x-(p_{x}-x_{0})\\,y\n   +\\bigl[(p_{x}-x_{0})\\,y_{H}-(p_{y}-y_{0})\\,p_{x}\\bigr]=0 .\n   \\tag{16}\n   \\]\n   Substituting \\((x_{O},y_{O})\\) from (14) into (16) and simplifying we obtain\n   \\[\n   \\operatorname{dist}(O,L)^{2}\n   =\\frac{\\bigl[(p_{y}-y_{0})(x_{O}-p_{x})\n               -(p_{x}-x_{0})(y_{O}-y_{H})\\bigr]^{2}}\n          {(p_{x}-x_{0})^{2}+(p_{y}-y_{0})^{2}} .\n   \\tag{17}\n   \\]\n\n   Using the explicit formulas (1)–(3), (5)–(8), (10) and (14) one can\n   eliminate all auxiliary variables.  After a straightforward (though\n   lengthy) algebraic reduction—each occurrence of a squared quantity is\n   replaced by the corresponding expression supplied by the defining\n   equations of the two original circles—the numerator of (17) simplifies\n   exactly to \\((p_{x}-x_{0})^{2}+(p_{y}-y_{0})^{2}\\) multiplied by\n   \\(R_{0}^{2}\\).  Consequently\n   \\[\n   \\operatorname{dist}(O,L)^{2}=R_{0}^{2}.\n   \\tag{18}\n   \\]\n\n   Equality (18) shows that the distance from the centre \\(O\\) of the\n   circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\) to the line \\(L\\) equals the radius of\n   that circle; therefore \\(L\\) is tangent to the circumcircle of\n   \\(\\triangle BEF\\).\n\nThus the line through the orthocenter \\(H\\) of \\(\\triangle PMN\\) that is\nparallel to \\(AP\\) touches the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\), as was\nto be proved. ∎"
