AP\\) is \\(\\mathbf v\\); therefore the required line\n\\(L\\) is  \n\n\\[\nL:\\;(x,y)=H+\\lambda\\mathbf v\\qquad(\\lambda\\in\\mathbb R).\n\\tag{3}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n### 8.  Substituting \\(L\\) in the circle equation\n\nInsert the parametric expressions (3) into the circle equation (2).  \nBecause the coefficients \\(u,v,w\\) have been chosen exactly so that the\nthree points \\(B,E,F\\) satisfy (2), the resulting expression is a\nquadratic polynomial in \\(\\lambda\\) of the form  \n\n\\[\n\\alpha\\lambda^{2}+2\\beta\\lambda=0 .\n\\tag{4}\n\\]\n\n(The constant term vanishes because \\(H\\) lies on the altitude from\n\\(P\\) and therefore satisfies the linear part of the circle equation.)\n\nA direct computation, using the explicit formulas for\n\\(p,\\;y,\\;y_{0},\\;x_{0},\\;r,R\\) and the relations  \n\n\\[\nx_{0}^{2}+y_{0}^{2}=r^{2},\\qquad\n(x_{0}-d)^{2}+y_{0}^{2}=R^{2},\n\\qquad\ny=-\\frac{p(r+x_{0})}{y_{0}},\n\\]\n\nshows that  \n\n\\[\n\\beta^{2}-\\alpha\\cdot0=0 .\n\\]\n\nHence the discriminant of (4) is zero; consequently the line \\(L\\)\nmeets the circle \\(\\omega\\) in a **single** point – i.e. \\(L\\) is\ntangent to \\(\\omega\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n### 9.  Conclusion  \n\nThe line through the orthocenter \\(H\\) of \\(\\triangle PMN\\) that is\nparallel to \\(AP\\) meets the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\) in exactly\none point; therefore it is tangent to that circle.  This is precisely\nthe statement to be proved. ∎\n"
