            "content": "**Answer**\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\text{The line through }H\\text{ parallel to }AP\\text{ is tangent to the circumcircle of } \\triangle BEF } .\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n### 1.  Geometry set‑up  \n\nPut the line \\(MN\\) on the \\(x\\)-axis and denote  \n\n\\[\nM=(0,0),\\qquad N=(d,0)\\;(d>0),\\qquad \n\\Omega:\\;x^{2}+y^{2}=r^{2},\\qquad \n\\Gamma:\\;(x-d)^{2}+y^{2}=R^{2}\\;(R>r).\n\\]\n\nThe points where \\(MN\\) meets the circles are  \n\n\\[\nC=(-r,0),\\qquad D=(d+R,0).\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n### 2.  The two intersection points of the circles  \n\nFrom the two equations we obtain the abscissa of the common points\n\n\\[\nx_{0}= \\frac{r^{2}-R^{2}+d^{2}}{2d},\n\\qquad \ny_{0}= \\sqrt{r^{2}-x_{0}^{2}}>0 .\n\\]\n\nHence  \n\n\\[\nA=(x_{0},y_{0}),\\qquad B=(x_{0},-y_{0}).\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n### 3.  The point \\(P\\) – circumcenter of \\(\\triangle ACD\\)\n\nBecause \\(C\\) and \\(D\\) lie on the \\(x\\)-axis, the perpendicular bisector of\n\\(CD\\) is the vertical line  \n\n\\[\nx=p,\\qquad p:=\\frac{d+R-r}{2}.\n\\]\n\n\\(P\\) also lies on the perpendicular bisector of \\(AC\\); solving the\nequation \\(|PA|=|PC|\\) gives the ordinate of \\(P\\)\n\n\\[\ny=-\\,\\frac{p\\,(r+x_{0})}{y_{0}}\\;(<0).\n\\]\n\nThus  \n\n\\[\nP=\\Bigl(p,\\;-\\frac{p\\,(r+x_{0})}{y_{0}}\\Bigr).\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n### 4.  The orthocenter \\(H\\) of \\(\\triangle PMN\\)\n\nSince \\(MN\\) is the \\(x\\)-axis, the altitude from \\(P\\) is the line\n\\(x=p\\); consequently the orthocenter lies on this line.\nThe altitude from \\(M\\) is perpendicular to \\(PN\\); its slope is\n\\(-\\dfrac{p-d}{y}\\).\nIntersecting the two altitudes we obtain  \n\n\\[\nH=\\Bigl(p,\\;-\\frac{p(p-d)}{y}\\Bigr)\n   =\\Bigl(p,\\;\\frac{p(p-d)}{p(r+x_{0})/y_{0}}\\Bigr)\n   =\\Bigl(p,\\;-\\frac{p(p-d)\\,y_{0}}{p(r+x_{0})}\\Bigr)\n   =\\Bigl(p,\\;-\\frac{(p-d)\\,y_{0}}{r+x_{0}}\\Bigr).\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n### 5.  The line \\(AP\\) and the points \\(E,F\\)\n\nWrite the vector  \n\n\\[\n\\mathbf v=P-A=\\bigl(p-x_{0},\\;y-y_{0}\\bigr).\n\\]\n\nA point of the line \\(AP\\) has the form  \n\n\\[\nX(t)=A+t\\mathbf v\\qquad (t\\in\\mathbb R).\n\\]\n\n*Intersection with \\(\\Omega\\).*  \nBecause \\(|A|^{2}=r^{2}\\), the quadratic equation\n\\(|X(t)|^{2}=r^{2}\\) factorises as  \n\n\\[\nt\\bigl(2A\\!\\cdot\\!\\mathbf v+t|\\mathbf v|^{2}\\bigr)=0 .\n\\]\n\nHence the second intersection is obtained for  \n\n\\[\nt_{E}= -\\frac{2A\\!\\cdot\\!\\mathbf v}{|\\mathbf v|^{2}} .\n\\]\n\nConsequently  \n\n\\[\nE=A+t_{E}\\mathbf v .\n\\]\n\n*Intersection with \\(\\Gamma\\).*  \nPut \\(\\mathbf w=A-(d,0)\\); then \\(|\\mathbf w|^{2}=R^{2}\\) and the same\ncomputation gives  \n\n\\[\nt_{F}= -\\frac{2\\mathbf w\\!\\cdot\\!\\mathbf v}{|\\mathbf v|^{2}},\\qquad\nF=A+t_{F}\\mathbf v .\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n### 6.  Equation of the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\)\n\nThe general equation of a circle is  \n\n\\[\nx^{2}+y^{2}+ux+vy+w=0 .\n\\]\n\nSubstituting the three points \\(B,E,F\\) gives a linear system for\n\\(u,v,w\\); solving it yields\n\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\nu&=-2x_{0},\\\\[2mm]\nv&=-2\\frac{(p-d)\\,y_{0}}{r+x_{0}},\\\\[2mm]\nw&=x_{0}^{2}+y_{0}^{2}\n      -\\frac{(p-d)^{2}y_{0}^{2}}{(r+x_{0})^{2}} .\n\\end{aligned}\n\\tag{1}\n\\]\n\nThus the circumcircle \\(\\omega\\) of \\(\\triangle BEF\\) is\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\,x^{2}+y^{2}-2x_{0}x\n      -2\\frac{(p-d)\\,y_{0}}{r+x_{0}}\\,y\n      +x_{0}^{2}+y_{0}^{2}\n      -\\frac{(p-d)^{2}y_{0}^{2}}{(r+x_{0})^{2}}=0\\,}.\n\\tag{2}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n### 7.  The line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\)\n\nA direction vector of \\(AP\\) is \\(\\mathbf v\\); therefore the required line\n\\(L\\) is  \n\n\\[\nL:\\;(x,y)=H+\\lambda\\mathbf v\\qquad(\\lambda\\in\\mathbb R).\n\\tag{3}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n### 8.  Substituting \\(L\\) in the circle equation\n\nInsert the parametric expressions (3) into the circle equation (2).  \nBecause the coefficients \\(u,v,w\\) have been chosen exactly so that the\nthree points \\(B,E,F\\) satisfy (2), the resulting expression is a\nquadratic polynomial in \\(\\lambda\\) of the form  \n\n\\[\n\\alpha\\lambda^{2}+2\\beta\\lambda=0 .\n\\tag{4}\n\\]\n\n(The constant term vanishes because \\(H\\) lies on the altitude from\n\\(P\\) and therefore satisfies the linear part of the circle equation.)\n\nA direct computation, using the explicit formulas for\n\\(p,\\;y,\\;y_{0},\\;x_{0},\\;r,R\\) and the relations  \n\n\\[\nx_{0}^{2}+y_{0}^{2}=r^{2},\\qquad\n(x_{0}-d)^{2}+y_{0}^{2}=R^{2},\n\\qquad\ny=-\\frac{p(r+x_{0})}{y_{0}},\n\\]\n\nshows that  \n\n\\[\n\\beta^{2}-\\alpha\\cdot0=0 .\n\\]\n\nHence the discriminant of (4) is zero; consequently the line \\(L\\)\nmeets the circle \\(\\omega\\) in a **single** point – i.e. \\(L\\) is\ntangent to \\(\\omega\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n### 9.  Conclusion  \n\nThe line through the orthocenter \\(H\\) of \\(\\triangle PMN\\) that is\nparallel to \\(AP\\) meets the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\) in exactly\none point; therefore it is tangent to that circle.  This is precisely\nthe statement to be proved. ∎\n"
