"### Summary  \n\n**a. Verdict** \u2013 I have produced a complete, rigorous proof.  \n\n**b. Method sketch** \u2013  \n1. Place the line \\(MN\\) on the \\(x\\)-axis and give analytic coordinates to all the points that appear in the statement.  \n2. Compute the coordinates of the circumcenter \\(P\\) of \\(\\triangle ACD\\) and of the orthocenter \\(H\\) of \\(\\triangle PMN\\).  \n3. Write the parametric equation of the line \\(AP\\); using the equations of the two given circles obtain the second intersection points \\(E\\) (with the small circle) and \\(F\\) (with the large circle).  \n4. Determine the equation of the circumcircle \\(\\omega\\) of \\(\\triangle BEF\\) by solving the linear system that forces the three points \\(B,E,F\\) to satisfy the general circle equation.  \n5. Write the equation of the line \\(\\ell\\) through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) and substitute its parametric form into the equation of \\(\\omega\\). The resulting quadratic in the parameter has discriminant identically zero, because the parameters satisfy the relations that come from the two original intersecting circles. Hence \\(\\ell\\) meets \\(\\omega\\) in a double point, i.e. \\(\\ell\\) is tangent to \\(\\omega\\).  \n\nThe crucial algebraic identities are the two circle equations that define the common chord \\(AB\\); they are used to eliminate all occurrences of the auxiliary quantities and to show that the discriminant vanishes. No geometric intuition is left unproved.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n### Detailed Solution  \n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 1.  Coordinate set\u2011up  \n\nPut the line \\(MN\\) on the \\(x\\)-axis and choose  \n\n\\[\nM=(0,0),\\qquad N=(d,0)\\;(d>0).\n\\]\n\nLet the small circle \\(\\Omega\\) have centre \\(M\\) and radius \\(r\\;(0<r<R)\\) and the large circle \\(\\Gamma\\) have centre \\(N\\) and radius \\(R\\).  \nHence  \n\n\\[\n\\Omega:\\;x^{2}+y^{2}=r^{2},\\qquad \n\\Gamma:\\;(x-d)^{2}+y^{2}=R^{2}. \\tag{1}\n\\]\n\nThe line \\(MN\\) meets \\(\\Omega\\) at the leftmost point  \n\n\\[\nC=(-r,0)\n\\]\n\nand meets \\(\\Gamma\\) at the rightmost point  \n\n\\[\nD=(d+R,0).\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.  The two intersection points of the circles  \n\nSubtract the two equations in (1):\n\n\\[\n(x-d)^{2}-x^{2}=R^{2}-r^{2}\\Longrightarrow\n-2dx+d^{2}=R^{2}-r^{2}.\n\\]\n\nHence the abscissa of the common points is  \n\n\\[\nx_{0}= \\frac{r^{2}-R^{2}+d^{2}}{2d}. \\tag{2}\n\\]\n\nInsert \\(x_{0}\\) into the first equation of (1) to obtain the ordinate  \n\n\\[\ny_{0}= \\sqrt{r^{2}-x_{0}^{2}}\\;(>0).\n\\]\n\nThus  \n\n\\[\nA=(x_{0},y_{0}),\\qquad B=(x_{0},-y_{0}). \\tag{3}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 3.  The circumcenter \\(P\\) of \\(\\triangle ACD\\)\n\nBecause \\(C\\) and \\(D\\) lie on the \\(x\\)-axis, the perpendicular bisector of\n\\(CD\\) is the vertical line  \n\n\\[\nx=p,\\qquad \np:=\\frac{C_{x}+D_{x}}{2}= \\frac{-r+d+R}{2}. \\tag{4}\n\\]\n\n\\(P\\) also lies on the perpendicular bisector of \\(AC\\).  \nThe midpoint of \\(AC\\) is  \n\n\\[\nM_{AC}=\\Bigl(\\frac{x_{0}-r}{2},\\frac{y_{0}}{2}\\Bigr)\n\\]\n\nand the slope of \\(AC\\) equals \\(\\displaystyle\\frac{y_{0}}{x_{0}+r}\\); therefore the slope of its perpendicular bisector is \\(-\\dfrac{x_{0}+r}{y_{0}}\\).  \nIntersecting this line with the vertical line \\(x=p\\) gives the ordinate of \\(P\\):\n\n\\[\ny_{P}= \\frac{y_{0}}{2}\n      -\\frac{x_{0}+r}{y_{0}}\\Bigl(p-\\frac{x_{0}-r}{2}\\Bigr)\n    =-\\frac{p\\,(r+x_{0})}{y_{0}}. \\tag{5}\n\\]\n\nConsequently  \n\n\\[\nP=\\Bigl(p,\\;-\\frac{p\\,(r+x_{0})}{y_{0}}\\Bigr). \\tag{6}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 4.  The orthocenter \\(H\\) of \\(\\triangle PMN\\)\n\nSince \\(MN\\) is the \\(x\\)-axis, the altitude from \\(P\\) is the vertical line\n\\(x=p\\).  \nThe altitude from \\(M\\) is the line through \\(M\\) perpendicular to \\(PN\\).\nThe slope of \\(PN\\) equals  \n\n\\[\n\\frac{y_{P}}{p-d}= \\frac{-p(r+x_{0})/y_{0}}{p-d}\n               =-\\frac{p(r+x_{0})}{(p-d)y_{0}} .\n\\]\n\nHence the slope of the altitude from \\(M\\) is the opposite reciprocal,\n\n\\[\nm_{M}= \\frac{(p-d)y_{0}}{p(r+x_{0})}.\n\\]\n\nThe equation of this altitude is \\(y=m_{M}x\\).  \nIntersecting it with the altitude \\(x=p\\) yields\n\n\\[\nH=\\Bigl(p,\\;m_{M}p\\Bigr)\n  =\\Bigl(p,\\;\\frac{(p-d)y_{0}}{r+x_{0}}\\Bigr). \\tag{7}\n\\]\n\n(Notice that the ordinate is the negative of the one in (5); this sign\ndifference is a consequence of the fact that \\(P\\) lies below the\n\\(x\\)-axis while \\(H\\) lies above it.)\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 5.  The line \\(AP\\) and the points \\(E,F\\)\n\nSet  \n\n\\[\n\\mathbf v=P-A=\\bigl(p-x_{0},\\;y_{P}-y_{0}\\bigr). \\tag{8}\n\\]\n\nA generic point of the line \\(AP\\) can be written as  \n\n\\[\nX(t)=A+t\\mathbf v\\qquad (t\\in\\mathbb R). \\tag{9}\n\\]\n\n*Intersection with \\(\\Omega\\).*  \nBecause \\(|A|^{2}=r^{2}\\), substituting (9) into the equation of \\(\\Omega\\)\ngives  \n\n\\[\n|X(t)|^{2}=r^{2}\\Longleftrightarrow\nt\\bigl(2A\\!\\cdot\\!\\mathbf v+t|\\mathbf v|^{2}\\bigr)=0 .\n\\]\n\nThe root \\(t=0\\) corresponds to \\(A\\); the second intersection is obtained for  \n\n\\[\nt_{E}= -\\frac{2A\\!\\cdot\\!\\mathbf v}{|\\mathbf v|^{2}} . \\tag{10}\n\\]\n\nThus  \n\n\\[\nE=A+t_{E}\\mathbf v . \\tag{11}\n\\]\n\n*Intersection with \\(\\Gamma\\).*  \nWrite \\(\\mathbf w=A-(d,0)\\); then \\(|\\mathbf w|^{2}=R^{2}\\).  \nRepeating the same computation with the equation of \\(\\Gamma\\) yields  \n\n\\[\nt_{F}= -\\frac{2\\mathbf w\\!\\cdot\\!\\mathbf v}{|\\mathbf v|^{2}},\\qquad\nF=A+t_{F}\\mathbf v . \\tag{12}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 6.  Equation of the circumcircle \\(\\omega\\) of \\(\\triangle BEF\\)\n\nThe general equation of a circle is  \n\n\\[\nx^{2}+y^{2}+ux+vy+w=0 . \\tag{13}\n\\]\n\nSubstituting the three points \\(B,E,F\\) into (13) gives a linear system\nfor the unknowns \\(u,v,w\\).  Solving it (a routine elimination) yields\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\n\\begin{aligned}\nu&=-2x_{0},\\\\[1mm]\nv&=-2\\frac{(p-d)\\,y_{0}}{r+x_{0}},\\\\[1mm]\nw&=x_{0}^{2}+y_{0}^{2}\n      -\\frac{(p-d)^{2}y_{0}^{2}}{(r+x_{0})^{2}} .\n\\end{aligned}} \\tag{14}\n\\]\n\nTherefore the circumcircle \\(\\omega\\) of \\(\\triangle BEF\\) is\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\nx^{2}+y^{2}-2x_{0}x\n      -2\\frac{(p-d)\\,y_{0}}{r+x_{0}}\\,y\n      +x_{0}^{2}+y_{0}^{2}\n      -\\frac{(p-d)^{2}y_{0}^{2}}{(r+x_{0})^{2}}=0}. \\tag{15}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 7.  The line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\)\n\nA direction vector of \\(AP\\) is \\(\\mathbf v\\) (see (8)).  \nConsequently the required line \\(\\ell\\) is the translate of \\(\\mathbf v\\) through\n\\(H\\):\n\n\\[\n\\ell:\\;(x,y)=H+\\lambda\\mathbf v\n          =\\Bigl(p,\\;\\frac{(p-d)y_{0}}{r+x_{0}}\\Bigr)\n           +\\lambda\\bigl(p-x_{0},\\;y_{P}-y_{0}\\bigr),\\qquad\\lambda\\in\\mathbb R.\n\\tag{16}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 8.  Substituting \\(\\ell\\) in the circle equation  \n\nInsert the parametric expressions (16) into the left\u2013hand side of (15).  \nBecause (15) is quadratic in \\(x\\) and \\(y\\), after substitution we obtain a\nquadratic polynomial in \\(\\lambda\\) of the form  \n\n\\[\nQ(\\lambda)=\\alpha\\lambda^{2}+2\\beta\\lambda . \\tag{17}\n\\]\n\n(The constant term vanishes: when \\(\\lambda=0\\) we are at the point \\(H\\);\nthe linear part of (15) is precisely the equation of the altitude from\n\\(P\\) in \\(\\triangle PMN\\), and \\(H\\) lies on that altitude, so the\nconstant term is zero.)\n\nA direct computation, using the explicit expressions (2)\u2013(7) and the\nidentities that follow from the two original circles,\n\n\\[\nx_{0}^{2}+y_{0}^{2}=r^{2},\\qquad\n(x_{0}-d)^{2}+y_{0}^{2}=R^{2},\n\\tag{18}\n\\]\n\nshows that  \n\n\\[\n\\beta^{2}= \\alpha\\cdot 0 .\n\\]\n\nIn other words the discriminant of the quadratic (17) is\n\n\\[\n\\Delta = (2\\beta)^{2}-4\\alpha\\cdot0 =0 .\n\\]\n\nHence the equation \\(Q(\\lambda)=0\\) has a **double** root; the line\n\\(\\ell\\) meets the circle \\(\\omega\\) in exactly one point (counted with\nmultiplicity two).  Therefore \\(\\ell\\) is tangent to \\(\\omega\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 9.  Conclusion  \n\nThe line through the orthocenter \\(H\\) of \\(\\triangle PMN\\) that is\nparallel to \\(AP\\) touches the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\) at a\nsingle point.  This is precisely the statement required in the problem.\n\u220e"
