t condition for ℓ to be tangent to the circumcircle of B,E,F, provided ℓ does not pass through B. Indeed, if ℓ is tangent at some point T on the circle, then angle between ℓ and BE equals angle BTE (since chord BE and tangent at T). Similarly, angle between BF and ℓ equals angle BTF. Since T is the midpoint of arc EF not containing B, arcs ET and FT are equal, so angles BTE = BTF. Conversely, if the equality holds, then ℓ must be tangent. The lemma's proof uses arcs and tangent-chord theorem. That seems valid.\n\nThus the solution's reasoning is valid.\n\nNow we need to check if any steps are missing or have justification gaps. The solution uses the existence of spiral similarity, which is a known fact: given two intersecting circles, there is a unique spiral similarity mapping one to the other, centered at either intersection point. This is a valid method. They also claim that AP passes through the centre of the similarity, thus invariant. Indeed, P is the circumcenter of triangle ACD. Does AP pass through the center of the spiral similarity? The center of the spiral similarity is A, not P. Wait, they say \"the line AP passes through the centre of the similarity, hence it is invariant under S.\" The centre of the similarity is A. AP passes through A by definition (since it's line through A and P). So any line through A is invariant under the spiral similarity? Not exactly: Under a spiral similarity centered at A, any point X on the line through A will map to a point X' also on the same line (collinear with A). So the line as a set is invariant. So AP is invariant as a line. That's correct.\n\nThus step 2 is correct.\n\nNow they claim that the orthocenter H of triangle PMN cannot coincide with B. They argue that if H = B, then BP ⟂ MN, but B lies on both circles, and chord AB is not perpendicular to MN. This is a bit hand-wavy but plausible: In a triangle PMN, the orthocenter H is the intersection of altitudes from P, M, N. If H = B, then B must lie on altitude from