us the four points \\(B, C, D, H\\) have equal subtended angles at the pairs \\((C,D)\\) and are therefore concyclic.  \nDenote their common circum\u2011circle by \\(\\omega\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 4.  Angles formed by the chords \\(B E\\) and \\(B F\\)\n\nThe spiral similarity \\(\\mathcal{S}\\) sends the chord \\(B E\\) of \\(\\Omega\\) to the chord \\(B F\\) of \\(\\Gamma\\).  \nHence the oriented angle between the two chords equals the oriented angle of the similarity:\n\n\\[\n\\angle (B E , B F)=\\angle C A D .\n\\tag{4.1}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 5.  The line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(A P\\) is tangent to \\((B E F)\\)\n\nLet \\(\\ell\\) be the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(A P\\); let \\(T\\neq B\\) be the second intersection of \\(\\ell\\) with the circum\u2011circle \\((B E F)\\).  \nWe shall prove that \\(\\ell\\) is tangent to \\((B E F)\\) at \\(T\\).\n\nBecause \\(\\ell\\parallel A P\\), the angle that \\(\\ell\\) makes with the chord \\(B T\\) equals the angle that \\(A P\\) makes with the same chord:\n\n\\[\n\\angle (\\ell , B T)=\\angle (A P , B T).\n\\tag{5.1}\n\\]\n\nFrom the cyclicity of \\(B C D H\\) we have  \n\n\\[\n\\angle B H T = \\angle B C D .\n\\tag{5.2}\n\\]\n\nUsing (1.3) and (3.1) we obtain  \n\n\\[\n\\angle B C D = \\angle C A D .\n\\tag{5.3}\n\\]\n\nCombining (5.2) and (5.3) gives  \n\n\\[\n\\angle B H T = \\angle C A D .\n\\tag{5.4}\n\\]\n\nSince \\(\\ell\\parallel A P\\) and \\(H\\in\\ell\\), the angle between \\(\\ell\\) and the chord \\(B T\\) equals \\(\\angle B H T\\); therefore, by (5.4),\n\n\\[\n\\angle (\\ell , B T)=\\angle C A D .\n\\tag{5.5}\n\\]\n\nFinally, by (4.1) the angle \\(\\angle C A D\\) coincides with the angle subtended by the chord \\(B E\\) (or \\(B F\\)) in the opposite arc of the circle \\((B E F)\\):\n\n\\[\n\\angle C A D = \\angle (B E , B F)=\\angle B T E .\n\\tag{5.6}\n\\]\n\nThus