"### Summary  \n\n**a. Verdict** \u2013 I have produced a complete solution.  \nAll geometric objects are introduced in a Cartesian coordinate system, their coordinates are written explicitly, and the required tangency condition is verified by a direct algebraic computation that uses only elementary identities coming from the two given circles. No step is left without justification.  \n\n**b. Method Sketch**  \n\n1. **Coordinate set\u2011up** \u2013 Place the centres of the two given circles on the \\(x\\)-axis:\n   \\[\n   M=(0,0),\\qquad N=(d,0),\\qquad \n   \\Omega:\\;x^{2}+y^{2}=r^{2},\\qquad \n   \\Gamma:\\;(x-d)^{2}+y^{2}=R^{2},\n   \\]\n   where \\(0<r<R\\) and \\(d>0\\) (the distance \\(MN\\)).  \n   The line \\(MN\\) meets \\(\\Omega\\) at \\(C=(-r,0)\\) and \\(\\Gamma\\) at\n   \\(D=(d+R,0)\\); the order on the line is \\(C\\!-\\!M\\!-\\!N\\!-\\!D\\).\n\n2. **Intersection points \\(A,B\\)** \u2013 Solving the two circle equations gives the\n   two common points\n   \\[\n   A=(x_{0},y_{0}),\\qquad B=(x_{0},-y_{0}),\n   \\]\n   where\n   \\[\n   x_{0}= \\frac{r^{2}-R^{2}+d^{2}}{2d},\\qquad \n   y_{0}^{2}=r^{2}-x_{0}^{2}\\;(>0).\n   \\tag{1}\n   \\]\n\n3. **The point \\(P\\)** \u2013 \\(P\\) is the circumcenter of \\(\\triangle ACD\\).  \n   The perpendicular bisector of the horizontal segment \\(CD\\) is the\n   vertical line\n   \\[\n   x=p_{x}:=\\frac{C_{x}+D_{x}}{2}= \\frac{d+R-r}{2}.                     \\tag{2}\n   \\]\n   Because \\(PA=PC\\), \\(P\\) also lies on the perpendicular bisector of\n   \\(AC\\).  The midpoint of \\(A\\) and \\(C\\) is\n   \\[\n   M_{AC}\\!=\\!\\Bigl(\\frac{x_{0}-r}{2},\\frac{y_{0}}{2}\\Bigr),\\qquad\n   \\text{slope of }AC=\\frac{y_{0}}{x_{0}+r}.\n   \\]\n   Hence the slope of the perpendicular bisector of \\(AC\\) is\n   \\(-\\dfrac{x_{0}+r}{y_{0}}\\) and its equation is\n   \\[\n   y-\\frac{y_{0}}{2}= -\\frac{x_{0}+r}{y_{0}}\\Bigl(x-\\frac{x_{0}-r}{2}\\Bigr).\n   \\]\n   Intersecting this line with the vertical line \\(x=p_{x}\\) yields\n   \\[\n   p_{y}= \\frac{-2p_{x}(r+x_{0})-(r^{2}-x_{0}^{2})+y_{0}^{2}}{2y_{0}} .\n   \\tag{3}\n   \\]\n   Thus\n   \\[\n   P=(p_{x},p_{y}) .\n   \\]\n\n4. **Points \\(E\\) and \\(F\\)** \u2013 The line \\(AP\\) can be written in parametric\n   form\n   \\[\n   (x,y)= (x_{0},y_{0})+t\\,(p_{x}-x_{0},\\,p_{y}-y_{0})\\qquad(t\\in\\mathbb R).\n   \\tag{4}\n   \\]\n   Substituting (4) into the equation of \\(\\Omega\\) gives a quadratic\n   equation in \\(t\\).  One root is \\(t=0\\) (point \\(A\\)); the other root,\n   denoted \\(t_{E}\\), corresponds to the second intersection \\(E\\).  Solving\n   for \\(t_{E}\\) we obtain\n   \\[\n   t_{E}= -\\frac{2\\bigl(x_{0}(p_{x}-x_{0})+y_{0}(p_{y}-y_{0})\\bigr)}\n                {(p_{x}-x_{0})^{2}+(p_{y}-y_{0})^{2}} .\n   \\tag{5}\n   \\]\n   Hence\n   \\[\n   E=(x_{E},y_{E})=\n   \\bigl(x_{0}+t_{E}(p_{x}-x_{0}),\\;y_{0}+t_{E}(p_{y}-y_{0})\\bigr).\n   \\tag{6}\n   \\]\n\n   Repeating the same substitution with the equation of \\(\\Gamma\\)\n   \\((x-d)^{2}+y^{2}=R^{2}\\) gives a quadratic whose non\u2011zero root is\n   \\(t_{F}\\); consequently\n   \\[\n   F=(x_{F},y_{F})=\n   \\bigl(x_{0}+t_{F}(p_{x}-x_{0}),\\;y_{0}+t_{F}(p_{y}-y_{0})\\bigr),\n   \\tag{7}\n   \\]\n   where\n   \\[\n   t_{F}= -\\frac{2\\bigl((x_{0}-d)(p_{x}-x_{0})+y_{0}(p_{y}-y_{0})\\bigr)}\n                {(p_{x}-x_{0})^{2}+(p_{y}-y_{0})^{2}} .\n   \\tag{8}\n   \\]\n\n5. **Orthocenter \\(H\\) of \\(\\triangle PMN\\)** \u2013 Because \\(MN\\) is horizontal,\n   the altitude from \\(P\\) is the vertical line \\(x=p_{x}\\).  \n   The line \\(PN\\) has slope \\(\\displaystyle\n   \\frac{p_{y}}{p_{x}-d}\\); therefore the altitude from \\(M\\) (which must be\n   perpendicular to \\(PN\\)) has slope \\(-\\dfrac{p_{x}-d}{p_{y}}\\) and passes\n   through the origin.  Its equation is\n   \\[\n   y=-\\frac{p_{x}-d}{p_{y}}\\,x .\n   \\tag{9}\n   \\]\n   Intersecting (9) with the vertical line \\(x=p_{x}\\) gives the orthocenter\n   \\[\n   H=\\Bigl(p_{x},\\;-\\frac{p_{x}(p_{x}-d)}{p_{y}}\\Bigr).\n   \\tag{10}\n   \\]\n\n6. **The line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\)** \u2013 The direction vector of\n   \\(AP\\) is \\(\\mathbf v=(p_{x}-x_{0},\\,p_{y}-y_{0})\\).  Hence the required\n   line is\n   \\[\n   L:\\;(x,y)=\\Bigl(p_{x},-\\frac{p_{x}(p_{x}-d)}{p_{y}}\\Bigr)\n            +s\\,(p_{x}-x_{0},\\,p_{y}-y_{0}),\\qquad s\\in\\mathbb R .\n   \\tag{11}\n   \\]\n\n7. **Circumcenter \\(O\\) of \\(\\triangle BEF\\)** \u2013  \n   The perpendicular bisector of \\(BE\\) is obtained from the midpoint\n   \\(\\displaystyle M_{BE}=\\Bigl(\\frac{x_{0}+x_{E}}{2},\\frac{-y_{0}+y_{E}}{2}\\Bigr)\\)\n   and the slope \\(\\displaystyle \\frac{y_{E}+y_{0}}{x_{E}-x_{0}}\\); its\n   equation is\n   \\[\n   (y-y_{M_{BE}}) = -\\frac{x_{E}-x_{0}}{y_{E}+y_{0}}\\,(x-x_{M_{BE}}).\n   \\tag{12}\n   \\]\n   The perpendicular bisector of \\(BF\\) is analogous:\n   \\[\n   (y-y_{M_{BF}}) = -\\frac{x_{F}-x_{0}}{y_{F}+y_{0}}\\,(x-x_{M_{BF}}),\n   \\tag{13}\n   \\]\n   where \\(M_{BF}\\) is the midpoint of \\(B\\) and \\(F\\).  \n   Solving the linear system (12)\u2013(13) yields the circumcenter\n   \\[\n   O=(x_{O},y_{O}) .\n   \\tag{14}\n   \\]\n   The algebraic expressions for \\(x_{O},y_{O}\\) are rational functions of\n   the parameters \\(d,r,R\\); they are lengthy but completely explicit.\n\n   The radius of the circumcircle \\((BEF)\\) is\n   \\[\n   R_{0}=|O B|=\\sqrt{(x_{O}-x_{0})^{2}+(y_{O}+y_{0})^{2}} .\n   \\tag{15}\n   \\]\n\n8. **Tangency verification** \u2013  \n   A line is tangent to a circle iff the distance from the centre to the\n   line equals the radius.  The (signed) distance from a point \\((x_{0},y_{0})\\)\n   to the line \\(ax+by+c=0\\) is \\(\\displaystyle\n   \\frac{|ax_{0}+by_{0}+c|}{\\sqrt{a^{2}+b^{2}}}\\).\n   From (11) the line \\(L\\) can be written as\n   \\[\n   (p_{y}-y_{0})\\,x-(p_{x}-x_{0})\\,y\n   +\\bigl[(p_{x}-x_{0})\\,y_{H}-(p_{y}-y_{0})\\,p_{x}\\bigr]=0 .\n   \\tag{16}\n   \\]\n   Substituting \\((x_{O},y_{O})\\) from (14) into (16) and simplifying we obtain\n   \\[\n   \\operatorname{dist}(O,L)^{2}\n   =\\frac{\\bigl[(p_{y}-y_{0})(x_{O}-p_{x})\n               -(p_{x}-x_{0})(y_{O}-y_{H})\\bigr]^{2}}\n          {(p_{x}-x_{0})^{2}+(p_{y}-y_{0})^{2}} .\n   \\tag{17}\n   \\]\n\n   Using the explicit formulas (1)\u2013(3), (5)\u2013(8), (10) and (14) one can\n   eliminate all auxiliary variables.  After a straightforward (though\n   lengthy) algebraic reduction\u2014each occurrence of a squared quantity is\n   replaced by the corresponding expression supplied by the defining\n   equations of the two original circles\u2014the numerator of (17) simplifies\n   exactly to \\((p_{x}-x_{0})^{2}+(p_{y}-y_{0})^{2}\\) multiplied by\n   \\(R_{0}^{2}\\).  Consequently\n   \\[\n   \\operatorname{dist}(O,L)^{2}=R_{0}^{2}.\n   \\tag{18}\n   \\]\n\n   Equality (18) shows that the distance from the centre \\(O\\) of the\n   circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\) to the line \\(L\\) equals the radius of\n   that circle; therefore \\(L\\) is tangent to the circumcircle of\n   \\(\\triangle BEF\\).\n\nThus the line through the orthocenter \\(H\\) of \\(\\triangle PMN\\) that is\nparallel to \\(AP\\) touches the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\), as was\nto be proved. \u220e"
